Sec. 70001 establishes that all references in this title to a "section" or other provision mean the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and it disables the IRC Section 15 rule that would otherwise adjust tax rates for changes made by this title.

Sec. 70101 makes the reduced individual income tax rates permanent (removing the January 1, 2026 sunset) and adjusts inflation indexing so that the 12% and 22% bracket thresholds are indexed using the chained CPI rules, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70102 makes the increased standard deduction permanent and raises the amounts to $23,625 for single filers and $15,750 for married filing separately (with corresponding joint amounts), effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

Sec. 70103 permanently eliminates the personal exemption deduction but creates a temporary $6,000 deduction for taxpayers (and spouses on joint returns) age 65 or older for years before 2029, phased out by 6% of modified AGI above $75,000 ($150,000 joint), and requires a social security number.

Sec. 70104 makes the expanded $2,200 child tax credit permanent, requires social security numbers for the taxpayer and each qualifying child, indexes the $1,400 refundable portion and $2,200 credit amount for inflation after 2024/2025, and treats omissions of correct SSNs as mathematical or clerical errors, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

Sec. 70105 increases the Section 199A qualified business income deduction phase-in thresholds to $75,000 ($150,000 joint), creates a minimum $400 deduction for taxpayers with at least $1,000 of active qualified business income in trades or businesses where they materially participate, indexes these amounts after 2026, and applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70106 permanently raises the estate and gift tax basic exclusion amount to $15,000,000 per person (indexed for inflation after 2025), effective for estates of decedents dying and gifts made after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70107 makes the increased alternative minimum tax exemption amounts permanent, modifies inflation adjustments (using 2017 for the $109,400/$70,300 amounts and 2025 for the $1,000,000 amount), and changes the phaseout for the $1,000,000 exemption to 50 percent, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70108 makes the $750,000 limitation on acquisition indebtedness for qualified residence interest permanent, removes the mortgage insurance premium treatment as interest, and applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70109 makes the limitation on personal casualty loss deductions permanent (allowing only losses attributable to federally declared disasters) and extends it to "State declared disasters" as defined by the Governor and Secretary, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70110 permanently disallows miscellaneous itemized deductions but creates a new above-the-line deduction for educator expenses (including coaches, interscholastic sports administrators, and nonathletic instructional equipment), effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70111 replaces the Pease limitation with a new Section 68 rule reducing itemized deductions by 2/37 of the lesser of the deductions or taxable income exceeding the 37% bracket threshold, applies after other limitations, and excludes this reduction from the Section 199A deduction computation, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70112 makes qualified transportation fringe benefits permanent, removes the bicycle commuting reimbursement exclusion, adjusts the inflation base year to 1997, and conforms Section 274, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70113 makes the limitation on moving expense deductions permanent and adds an allowance for intelligence community employees (other than Armed Forces members) who relocate pursuant to a change in assignment, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70114 limits wagering loss deductions to 90 percent of losses and only to the extent of wagering gains, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70115 makes the increased ABLE account contribution limitation permanent (using the 1996 base year for the gift tax exclusion) and removes the 2026 sunset, effective for contributions made after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70116 extends the saver's credit for ABLE contributions permanently, increases the credit amount from $2,000 to $2,100 for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2026, and repeals the SECURE 2.0 coordination provision, effective for taxable years ending after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70117 makes permanent the ability to roll over qualified tuition program (529) funds to ABLE accounts, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70118 makes permanent the tax treatment of individuals performing services in the Sinai Peninsula and adds Kenya, Mali, Burkina Faso, and Chad as qualified hazardous duty areas, effective January 1, 2026.

Sec. 70119 extends and modifies the exclusion from gross income for student loans discharged on account of death or disability (including private education loans), requires the taxpayer's social security number, and applies to discharges after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70120 replaces the $10,000 state and local tax (SALT) deduction cap with an "applicable limitation amount" of $40,000 for 2025, $40,400 for 2026, 101% annual increases through 2029, and $10,000 after 2029, phased down by 30% of modified AGI above $500,000 (2025) or $505,000 (2026) with annual increases, but not below $10,000, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

Sec. 70201 creates a new above-the-line deduction for qualified tips (up to $25,000, phased out by $100 per $1,000 of modified AGI above $150,000/$300,000 joint) for tips received in occupations that customarily received tips before December 31, 2024, excludes specified service trades or businesses, requires an SSN, extends the tip credit to beauty services, adds reporting requirements, and sunsets after December 31, 2028, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

Sec. 70202 creates a new above-the-line deduction for qualified overtime compensation (up to $12,500/$25,000 joint, phased out by $100 per $1,000 of modified AGI above $150,000/$300,000 joint) required under the Fair Labor Standards Act, excludes qualified tips, requires an SSN, adds W-2 and 1099 reporting, and sunsets after December 31, 2028, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

Sec. 70203 allows a deduction for qualified passenger vehicle loan interest (up to $10,000, phased out by $200 per $1,000 of modified AGI above $100,000/$200,000 joint) for loans incurred after December 31, 2024, secured by a first lien on a vehicle with a GVWR under 14,000 pounds assembled in the United States, requires the VIN, excludes fleet/commercial/lease/salvage loans and related-party debt, and applies for taxable years 2025 through 2028.

Sec. 70204 establishes tax-exempt "Trump accounts" (treated like traditional IRAs) for children under 18, allowing contributions up to $5,000 per year (indexed after 2027) with no deduction, prohibiting distributions before age 18 except for rollovers, ABLE rollovers at age 17, excess contributions, or death, allowing employer contributions up to $2,500 excluded from income, and creating a $1,000 pilot program payment for children born 2025-2028, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025, with $410 million appropriated through 2034.

Sec. 70301 makes 100% bonus depreciation permanent for qualified property acquired after January 19, 2025, removes the phase-down percentages, allows a transitional 40%/60% election for property placed in service in the first taxable year ending after January 19, 2025, and extends 100% expensing to specified plants planted or grafted after that date.

Sec. 70302 creates a new Section 174A allowing a current deduction for domestic research or experimental expenditures (excluding foreign research) paid or incurred in taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024, with an elective 60-month amortization, conforms Section 174 to cover only foreign research over 15 years, and provides transition rules including a retroactive election for small businesses to 2021.

Sec. 70303 modifies the business interest limitation under Section 163(j) by removing the EBITDA-based adjusted taxable income rule (requiring EBIT-based computation) and expands floor plan financing to include trailers and campers, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

Sec. 70304 extends and enhances the paid family and medical leave credit by allowing employers to elect a credit based on insurance premiums, modifying the rate determination, changing the aggregation rule, allowing 6-month minimum leave policies, defining part-time employees (20+ hours/week), and coordinating with Section 280C, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70305 creates exceptions to the 50% limitation on business meal deductions for meals provided on certain fishing vessels or at fish processing facilities north of 50 degrees latitude outside metropolitan statistical areas, effective for amounts paid or incurred after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70306 increases the Section 179 expensing limits to $2,500,000 (from $1,000,000) and the phase-out threshold to $4,000,000 (from $2,500,000), with inflation adjustments using 2024/2025 base years, effective for property placed in service in taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

Sec. 70307 creates a new Section 168(n) allowing 100% special depreciation for qualified production property (nonresidential real property used in manufacturing/production/refining, placed in service in the U.S. before 2031, construction beginning after January 19, 2025 and before 2029), with recapture over 10 years, an election requirement, and treating such property as Section 1245 property, effective for property placed in service after enactment.

Sec. 70308 increases the advanced manufacturing investment credit from 25% to 35%, effective for property placed in service after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70309 treats spaceports like airports for tax-exempt facility bond rules, defines spaceport facilities (manufacturing, flight control, launch/reentry services, crew/cargo transfer), allows public use requirements to be waived, permits ground leases from the U.S., and creates an exception to the federally guaranteed bond prohibition, effective for obligations issued after enactment.

Sec. 70311 modifies the foreign tax credit limitation by allocating the Section 250 deduction (and related taxes) to foreign source net CFC tested income, disallowing interest and research expense allocation to such income, and making conforming changes, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70312 increases the deemed paid foreign tax credit from 80% to 90% (and the Section 78 gross-up), disallows 10% of foreign taxes on distributions of previously taxed net CFC tested income, and applies the disallowance to taxes paid after June 28, 2025.

Sec. 70313 sources income from the sale of U.S.-produced inventory through foreign branches as foreign source (up to 50% of such income) when the U.S. person maintains a foreign office, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70321 reduces the Section 250 deduction rates to 33.34% for foreign-derived deduction eligible income and 40% for net CFC tested income, repeals the 37.5%/50% rates, and applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70322 modifies the determination of deduction eligible income by including income from sales/dispositions of intangible property and depreciable property (effective for dispositions after June 16, 2025) and limits expense apportionment to properly allocable expenses other than interest and research (effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025).

Sec. 70323 renames global intangible low-taxed income as "net CFC tested income," repeals the 10% deemed return on foreign investments (subsections (b) and (d) of Section 951A), renames foreign-derived intangible income as "foreign-derived deduction eligible income," and applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70331 increases the base erosion minimum tax rate from 10% to 10.5%, eliminates the 2% increase for certain taxpayers, redesignates paragraphs, and makes conforming changes, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70341 coordinates the business interest limitation with interest capitalization provisions by applying the Section 163(j) limitation to business interest regardless of capitalization, applying allowed amounts first to capitalized interest, excluding capitalized interest from business interest, and providing regulatory authority, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70342 modifies the definition of adjusted taxable income for the business interest limitation by adding back Section 951(a), 951A(a), and 78 inclusions (and related Section 245A/250 deductions), effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70351 makes permanent the look-thru rule for related controlled foreign corporations (removing the January 1, 2026 sunset), effective for taxable years of foreign corporations beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70352 repeals the election for a 1-month deferral in determining the taxable year of specified foreign corporations, effective for taxable years beginning after November 30, 2025, with transition rules for changing taxable years.

Sec. 70353 restores the limitation on downward attribution of stock ownership (Section 318(a)(3) not applied to treat U.S. persons as owning stock owned by non-U.S. persons) and creates new Section 951B taxing foreign controlled United States shareholders of foreign controlled foreign corporations, effective for taxable years of foreign corporations beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70354 modifies the pro rata share rules under Section 951(a) to include subpart F income based on stock ownership on any day during the CFC year (rather than only the last day), includes Section 956 amounts on the last day the corporation is a CFC, coordinates with Section 951A tested income, and applies to taxable years of foreign corporations beginning after December 31, 2025, with a transition rule for dividends paid before 2026. [end of text]


